---
/** The support URL the App Store listing requires, answering the questions the app actually
 * generates, in the order they come up. */
import Page from "../../../layouts/Page.astro";
import { REPO_URL, SUPPORT_EMAIL } from "../../../lib/nx-link";

const title = "NX Link — Support";
const description =
  "How to connect an NX300 or NX3000 to NX Link, what to do when the camera does not appear, and where to report a problem.";
---

<Page title={title} description={description} path="/apps/nx-link/support">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>NX Link — Support</h1>

    <h2>Connecting, in three steps</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        On the camera: open the wireless menu, choose <strong>Link</strong>, then{" "}
        <strong>Select Files from Smartphone</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>
        On the iPhone: <strong>Settings › Wi-Fi</strong>, join the network whose name starts with
        <code>AP_SSC_NX</code>. It has no password.
      </li>
      <li>Open NX Link. It finds the camera and connects on its own.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      That network has no internet, by design. iOS will say so, and may offer to switch you back to
      a network that does — stay on the camera's one until the transfer has finished.
    </p>

    <h2>The camera does not appear</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Check the phone is on the camera's network.</strong> iOS switches away from a
        network with no internet more eagerly than you would expect, especially after a lock.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The camera gives up waiting.</strong> It switches its Wi-Fi off if nothing connects
        within a minute or two. Start the mode on the camera again and reopen the app promptly.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Allow the Local Network prompt.</strong> If it was declined, the app cannot reach
        the camera at all: Settings › NX Link › Local Network.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Use the right mode.</strong> Remote Viewfinder connects, but the camera will not
        share its card in that mode. For transfers it has to be Select Files from Smartphone.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Photos are missing from the grid</h2>
    <p>
      These cameras never offer RAW files over Wi-Fi. Only JPEGs are indexed, and only indexed files
      can be listed — so a camera set to RAW only looks like a camera with an empty card. Set it to
      JPEG or RAW+JPEG, and the photos you have already taken as RAW will still need the card
      reader. This is firmware behaviour, confirmed by reading the index on the card itself; no app
      can work around it.
    </p>

    <h2>A transfer is slow</h2>
    <p>
      A 20-megapixel original is around 8 MB and the camera's radio sets the pace: expect a few
      seconds each. The camera also serves one request at a time, so the app queues deliberately
      rather than asking for several at once — which is what makes the firmware drop the connection.
      Leave the screen on while a batch runs.
    </p>

    <h2>Shooting from the phone says the shot failed</h2>
    <p>
      The camera is focus-priority: if autofocus cannot lock, it refuses the shot and reports it.
      Point at something with more contrast, or switch the lens to manual focus and focus it
      yourself. Remote zoom needs a power-zoom lens — the 16–50 PZ responds, the manual-zoom lenses
      have no motor to drive.
    </p>

    <h2>Report a problem</h2>
    <p>
      Open an issue at <a href={`${REPO_URL}/issues`}>{REPO_URL}/issues</a>, or write to
      <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a>. Expect a reply within a few days;
      this is a free app maintained by one person.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a connection problem, the app's <strong>Troubleshooting</strong> screen (the ⋯ menu)
      produces exactly the detail needed — the addresses it tried and what answered. Paste it into
      the issue.
    </p>

    <h2>Another NX camera</h2>
    <p>
      Nothing in the app is written for a particular model: it reads what the camera declares and
      adapts. The NX300 and NX3000 are the two it has been tested against, and reports from other
      bodies are genuinely useful — the troubleshooting screen's output is the place to start.
    </p>
  </article>
</Page>

<style>
  .prose h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 4vw, 2.1rem);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    font-weight: 650;
    color: var(--rx-fg);
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }
  .prose {
    max-width: var(--rx-max);
  }
</style>
